Toroid Macabre
Ingredients: NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Organ + 1980s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The scene took root inside the circular concrete vacuum tunnel of a decommissioned 1970s particle accelerator converted into an illicit subterranean nightclub.

Toroid Macabre is a brooding, syncopated collision of smoky modal jazz and cold European post-punk dread. Towering, overdriven tonewheel and transistor organs snake through cavernous industrial spaces, trading sinister minor-scale improvisations with melancholic muted brass, all anchored by brittle 1980s drum machines that twitch with off-kilter broken-beat swing.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 84–104 BPM; unhurried yet tense, fusing off-beat, syncopated nu-jazz swing with the stiff, robotic clocking of 1980s programmable drum computers.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark modal frameworks dominated by Harmonic Minor, Phrygian Dominant, and Locrian modes, underpinned by creeping chromatic bass descents and dissonant organ clusters featuring diminished fifths.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Rotary Chamber Slap: Running high-drawbar Hammond and Vox Continental organs through overdriven Leslie cabinets placed inside subterranean concrete chambers, gating the decay for sudden drops into total vacuum.
  2. Gated FM Low-End: Layering woody acoustic upright bass with cold, metallic Yamaha DX7 bass patches ducked violently behind dry, papery snare hits.
  3. Doppler Flutter: Utilizing vintage tape delays and circular panning to mimic the psychoacoustic latency of sound racing endlessly around a curved particle ring.
- Dynamics: Starts as an asphyxiating crawl with solitary bass and hushed ride cymbals, steadily accumulating layers of snarling organ registration and muted brass before bursting into a blinding wall of gothic fortissimo, instantly choked back down to a shuddering sub-frequency hum.

Scene & Origin Lore
In late 1986, outside the French-Swiss border, trespassers breached the subterranean service ring of a decommissioned particle collider, christening the circular tunnel Le Faisceau (The Beam). Stripped of its electromagnets, the circular concrete corridor provided a continuous two-kilometer acoustic loop where illegal raves were held under generator power. Disaffected conservatory jazz dropouts and dyed-black gothic post-punks converged in this freezing, ozone-scented trench. Patrons wore tailored mourning coats reinforced with lead radiation aprons, kohl-stained eyelids hidden behind surplus laboratory safety goggles, and scuffed jazz Oxfords. Rather than dancing in static crowds, clubgoers were forced to promenade continuously along the curving concrete floor, drifting through shifting acoustic nodes where bands set up organs and drum machines at fixed intervals, letting the sound chase the audience through the dark.

Typical Instruments: Hammond B3 (with overdriven Leslie speaker), Vox Continental Transistor Organ, Roland TR-707 and LinnDrum computers, Harmon-muted trumpet, Yamaha DX7 synthesizer, acoustic upright bass (bowed and plucked), Baritone saxophone, Lexicon 224 digital reverb unit.

For Fans Of: Bohren & der Club of Gore, Tuxedomoon, Portishead, The Comet Is Coming

